kage remains.\n2. Every image classified as containing non-validated crypto has a documented remediation approach with rationale.\n3. The build-attestation source for every in-scope image is identified, or an explicit gap is recorded for follow-up.\n4. The module inventory is delivered in a structured form the compliance evidence package can consume without re-work.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P0, partial): produces the module inventory that is a component of the compliance evidence package.\n- Enables HLR-1 (P0): determines the remediation scope and approach for all serving-stack images.\n\nHmm wait — \"No cross-references to sibling epics\" — \"compliance evidence package\" as a concept is fine (it's the strategy's concept). OK.\n\n**E002 body:**\n\n# FIPS crypto remediation of KServe component images\n\n## Description\nMake every KServe component image run correctly on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters using validated cryptographic modules, with no non-validated crypto in the inference request path. Includes authoring and verifying the FIPS regression gating check that covers KServe image builds.\n\n## Scope\n- Apply the remediation approach determined by the serving-stack crypto audit to each KServe component image identified as linking non-validated crypto (rebuild against validated modules, version changes, or library replacement as documented).\n- Verify remediated images on a FIPS-enabled OpenShift cluster: the inference request path (InferenceService deployment and endpoint) exercises only validated crypto.\n- Author and verify the FIPS regression gating check covering KServe image builds: the check fails any KServe build that reintroduces non-validated crypto usage.\n- Update the module inventory entries for the remediated KServe images to reflect their post-remediation state, for consumption by the compliance evidence package.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n1. A post-remediation scan of every KServe component image shows no non-validated cryptographic library in the request path.\n2. Remediated images build green in the component build pipeline.\n3. Remediated images are verified to serve inference requests correctly on a FIPS-enabled OpenShift cluster.\n4. The FIPS regression gating check is active in the KServe build pipeline and demonstrably fails a build that reintroduces non-validated crypto usage (verified against a known-bad case) while passing the remediated builds.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): KServe component images run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto modules.\n- HLR-3 (P1, bundled): the CI FIPS regression check for KServe builds is authored and verified here — it shares the same build surfaces and remediated images as the HLR-1 work, so it is not separately schedulable.\n\n**E003 body:** analogous for Platform Operator images (operator image(s) run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters; gating check for operator builds).\n\nNote: operator is control plane — \"no non-validated crypto in the request path\" — HLR-1's request-path clause applies to serving path; for operator: \"run correctly on FIPS-enabled clusters using validated crypto\". OK.\n\n**E004 body:** analogous for serving data plane runtime images (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit class model servers). Request path clause applies.\n\n**E005 body:**\n\n# FIPS compliance evidence package assembly and submission\n\n## Description\nAssemble the FIPS 140-3 compliance evidence package for the model serving stack — module inventory, build attestations, and FIPS-mode test results — and submit it through the security review board to the external accredited certification laboratory.\n\n## Scope\n- Collect the module inventory from the serving-stack crypto audit (post-remediation state).\n- Collect build attestations for every in-scope serving-stack image from its identified build pipeline.\n- Collect FIPS-mode verification results (cluster verification of remediated images).\n- Assemble and internally validate the package: every inventory line backed by attestations and test results; gaps explicitly listed.\n- Submit the package through the security review board process at the defined milestones to the external certification laboratory.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n1. The evidence package contains the complete post-remediation module inventory, build attestations for every in-scope serving-stack image, and FIPS-mode test results.\n2. The package is internally validated: every module-inventory entry has corresponding attestations and test results; no silently missing evidence.\n3. The package is submitted through the security review board to the external certification laboratory, and the submission is acknowledged.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n- Certification laboratory turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security review board cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort; they dominate the program schedule but do not gate the engineering work in this strategy.\n- Certification outcome timing is outside engineering control; this epic's engineering completes at submission and triage.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P0): evidence package assembled and submitted through the security review board to the external certification lab.\n\n**E006 body:**\n\n# Document the model serving stack FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites\n\n## Description\nAuthor user-facing documentation stating the FIPS support posture of the model serving stack and the cluster prerequisites for running it on FIPS-enabled OpenShift clusters.\n\n## Scope\nContent outline (inside Scope):\n- FIPS support posture of the model serving stack: which surfaces (KServe components, platform operator, serving data plane model-server runtimes) are supported on FIPS-enabled clusters and to what degree.\n- Cluster prerequisites: FIPS-enabled OpenShift cluster configuration and any installation constraints.\n- Guidance on interpreting the compliance status and the certification submission state.\n- 