{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c7d016cde1faa10","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9013\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# RHAISTRAT-9013 — Decomposition: FIPS 140-3 compliance for the model serving stack\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E001 | Serving-stack crypto module audit and FIPS remediation plan | Investigation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E002 | FIPS crypto remediation of KServe component images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E003 | FIPS crypto remediation of Platform Operator images | Implementation | RHOAI Platform | Platform Operator | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E004 | FIPS crypto remediation of serving data plane runtime images | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | Serving Runtimes | P0 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E005 | FIPS compliance evidence package assembly and submission | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | KServe | P0 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9013-E006 | Document the model serving stack FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | Documentation | P0 |\n22\t\n23\tComponent mapping notes (canonical names from `.context/rhai-components.txt`):\n24\t\n25\t- The crypto audit spans all serving-stack surfaces (KServe components, Platform Operator, serving data plane runtimes); it is filed under the single most-relevant canonical component `KServe` (the serving stack core per architecture context).\n26\t- The evidence-package epic likewise spans the whole serving stack and is filed under `KServe`.\n27\t- The serving data plane model-server runtimes map to the canonical component `Serving Runtimes` — see Health Warnings for why this surface is included although the strategy's Affected Components list omits it.\n28\t- `Platform Operator` is used verbatim from the canonical list.\n29\t\n30\t## Dependency DAG\n31\t\n32\t```mermaid\n33\tgraph TD\n34\t    E001[\"E001 crypto audit (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 KServe image remediation\"]\n35\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Platform Operator image remediation\"]\n36\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004 data plane runtime image remediation\"]\n37\t    E001 --> E005[\"E005 evidence package\"]\n38\t    E002 --> E005\n39\t    E003 --> E005\n40\t    E004 --> E005\n41\t    E002 --> E006[\"E006 FIPS posture docs\"]\n42\t    E003 --> E006\n43\t    E004 --> E006\n44\t    E005 --> E006\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E005 → E006 (4 epics).\n48\t\n49\t## DAG Justification\n50\t\n51\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n52\t|------|------|-----------|\n53\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates Implementation) | The audit determines which KServe images link non-validated crypto and the remediation approach per image — it changes what the remediation epic does (true gate, `rewrite`). |\n54\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 | Same for Platform Operator images: scope and approach are outputs of the audit (`rewrite`). |\n55\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 | Same for serving data plane runtime images; the audit also confirms which data-plane images are affected at all (`rewrite`; narrows to verification-only if the data plane is found compliant). |\n56\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 13 (artifact consumption) | The evidence package directly consumes the audit's structured module inventory as one of its own components — a direct deliverable input, not merely an enabler of the remediation epics. |\n57\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 13 | The evidence package consumes the build attestations and FIPS-mode test results produced by the KServe remediation; serial. |\n58\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 13 | Same for the Platform Operator remediation outputs. |\n59\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 13 | Same for the serving data plane remediation outputs. |\n60\t| E002, E003, E004, E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy — documentation describes what was built and the final FIPS posture. These edges do not push priority upstream (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n61\t| (absent) E002 ↔ E003 ↔ E004 | Rules 14/15 | The three remediation surfaces are different components in different repos with no shared build artifacts — they run in parallel once the audit lands. |\n62\t| (absent) any konflux-onboarding edges | Rules 10/20 | No onboarding chain: every surface already builds in an existing pipeline (KServe is Konflux-onboarded per architecture context; attestation sources for the other surfaces are confirmed by the audit). |\n63\t\n64\tNo conditional branch files: the audit's outcomes are per-image findings (an unbounded set), not ≤3 discrete structural alternatives. The per-surface epic structure already absorbs the possible outcomes; each gated epic records its `gate_failure_impact` (all `rewrite`) in frontmatter.\n65\t\n66\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n67\t\n68\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n69\t|-----|----------|---------|\n70\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002, E003, E004 — each epic remediates one surface of \"all serving-stack images\" (KServe components, Platform Operator, serving data plane runtimes); full request-path coverage requires all three. |\n71\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E001 (produces the module inventory), E005 (assembles the package with attestations and test results and submits it through the security review board to the external certification lab). |\n72\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E002, E003, E004 (bundled) — the CI FIPS regression gating check is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable: each remediation epic authors and verifies the check covering its own component's builds, because the check spans exactly the same build surfaces and remediated images as the HLR-1 work; a standalone CI-check epic would be a work-type extraction auditing the same surfaces twice and is not separately schedulable. |\n73\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E006 — documentation of the FIPS support posture and cluster prerequisites. |\n74\t\n75\tAll four HLRs are covered; none orphaned.\n76\t\n77\t## Health Warnings\n78\t\n79\t1. **Missing strategy sections.** Affected Components is a bare list without the what-changes/owner-team columns; there are no Impacted Teams, Dependencies, Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then), or Non-Functional Requirements sections. Decomposition proceeded from the problem statement and HLRs. Team assignments are inferred: RHOAI Serving for KServe and serving data plane surfaces (owning team per `architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md`); RHOAI Platform is **assumed** for Platform Operator (unconfirmed — no Impacted Teams table to verify against); the docs epic is assigned to the serving team in the absence of a named docs team.\n80\t2. **Scope discrepancy between Affected Components and the problem statement (scope trap).** The problem statement names \"the serving data plane\" as linking non-validated crypto, but the Affected Components list omits Serving Runtimes; conversely, Platform Operator is listed as affected but is not named in the crypto sentence. HLR-1's scope (\"all serving-stack images … no non-validated crypto in the request path\") requires the union: KServe components, Platform Operator, and the serving data plane model servers — which per architecture context are the Serving Runtimes (vLLM, OpenVINO, Caikit-class runtimes). A remediation epic was created per surface (E002/E003/E004). Confirm the intended surface set with the strategy author; if the data plane is out of scope, E004 should be removed and HLR-1's request-path clause restated.\n81\t3. **Architecture-context coverage gap.** Only `architecture/rhoai-3.0/kserve.md` is provisioned — no context file exists for Platform Operator, Serving Runtimes, or Documentation. Both components are treated as in-platform on the strength of the strategy's own description (images already build and link crypto libraries; \"no compliance evidence package\" is the gap, not the components), so no onboarding chain was generated. The architecture-claims AI signal is scored 0 for epics whose components lack context coverage. If Platform Operator is genuinely absent from the platform inventory, a repo/license/Konflux onboarding chain would be required and this decomposition must be revised.\n82\t4. **External schedule risk (deliberately not modeled as ACs or DAG edges).** Certification-lab turnaround (historically six to nine months) and security review board cadence are externally controlled and cannot be compressed by engineering effort. They are recorded as Risks/Notes on the evidence-package epic; that epic's acceptance criteria end at submission through the security review board — the completion point engineering controls — while lab acknowledgment and triage are externally controlled outcomes tracked as schedule risk, not completion conditions. No epic models lab timing because no engineering work is gated by it.\n83\t5. **Priority notes (rule-derived, not inversions).** HLR-3 (P1) is kept bundled inside the P0 remediation epics per the shared-surface enforcement rule — recorded per-row in the traceability matrix above. The docs epic carries P0 although HLR-4 is P2: per the docs-authoring priority exception its priority derives from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0.\n84\t\n85\t## Tiered Delivery\n86\t\n87\tNo external *code* dependency exists in this strategy — no upstream PR/RFC, no vendor component gates engineering work. The external accredited laboratory is a submission target with externally controlled turnaround, not a build dependency. Partial value therefore ships without it: HLR-1 (FIPS-correct serving-stack images) and HLR-3 (CI regression enforcement) complete independently of certification timing, and HLR-2's engineering completes at submission. No Tier 1/Tier 2 epic split is required. Re-evaluate if security review board milestones are later found to gate the start of remediation phases rather than the submission.\n88\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f01b0db78708ef7","agent_id":"a16cf4daefa930ddb","session_id":"b04d48ec-e92b-4b9b-98a0-90c74f405f25","uuid":"174b29e7-18f7-45bd-a821-e254b87bf0bb","timestamp":"2026-10-26T19:15:31.620Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-8c7d016cde1faa10","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9013"}
